-- ===========================================================================
-- NORNE -- the Equinor field model, run through NVRS-CUDA.
--
-- The field
-- ---------
-- Norne lies in blocks 6608/10 and 6508/1 in the southern Norwegian Sea, on
-- the Nordland Ridge about 200 km off Helgeland, in roughly 380 m of water.
-- Discovered in 1991, on stream in November 1997 from the Norne FPSO, and
-- operated by Equinor (Statoil at first oil) on behalf of the licence.  It is
-- the reason this deck exists as a public benchmark at all: in 2001 the
-- operator and its partners released the full history-matched model, and it
-- has been the open reference case for reservoir simulation ever since.  The
-- deck read here is the version distributed with OPM.
--
-- Reservoir and rock
-- ------------------
-- The oil sits in Lower and Middle Jurassic sandstones, some 2,500-2,700 m
-- subsea, in a tilted fault block.  Four formations carry it, and their
-- contrast is the whole character of the model:
--
--   Garn      Middle Jurassic, the best of them -- clean, well sorted shallow
--             marine sand, permeability into the darcies.
--   Ile       beneath Garn, tidal to shoreface, good but more layered; the
--             vertical barriers between its subunits steer where gas goes.
--   Tofte     coarser, more heterogeneous, and the reason this deck carries
--             per-layer PERMZ multipliers rather than one KZMULT.
--   Tilje     the deepest, tidal and strongly heterolithic: thin sand-shale
--             interbedding, and by far the poorest connected of the four.
--
-- Porosity runs roughly 25-30% in Garn and Ile and falls into the teens in
-- Tilje; permeability spans about four orders of magnitude across the model.
-- That range is not decoration -- it is what makes the pressure operator hard
-- and what the multigrid in stage one of the CPR has to coarsen through.
--
-- Structure
-- ---------
-- The field is cut by a set of mostly N-S normal faults, 61 of them named in
-- this deck.  They matter twice over.  Geometrically, a throw juxtaposes one
-- column against parts of several others, so a cell can have any number of
-- neighbours -- degree runs from 1 to 17 here, mean 5.81 -- which is why the
-- assembly uses a general connection list and not a six-face stencil.
-- Dynamically, the fault transmissibility multipliers and MULTREGT between
-- fault blocks decide which parts of the field talk to each other at all:
-- 13,479 connections are scaled by named faults, 12,992 more by MULTREGT, and
-- 3,843 are sealed outright.
--
-- Production
-- ----------
-- Norne is produced by water injection with gas reinjection into the gas cap,
-- through horizontal and near-horizontal wells -- 24 of the 36 here have
-- horizontal sections.  The history in this deck runs from November 1997 to
-- December 2006, 3,304 days over 247 report steps, and the wells are worked
-- hard: C-1H, C-3H, C-4H and C-4AH alternate between water and gas injection
-- repeatedly, which is why the run banners a change of role rather than
-- logging it.  That alternation is the hardest thing in the schedule for a
-- solver, and it is exactly the part a synthetic benchmark never has.
--
-- The grid, the faults, the wells and the initial state all come from the
-- corner-point deck named by FIELDDECK.  That file is Equinor's own, read
-- unmodified; nothing in it is converted or rewritten beforehand.  What this
-- deck supplies is only what a corner-point deck states in tables NVRS does
-- not yet interpret: the fluid properties, the saturation functions, and the
-- solver settings.
--
-- Fetch the model first:
--     ./benchmark/get_norne.sh
--
-- Then:
--     NGPU=1 ./scripts/run.sh decks/NORNE.DATA
--
-- Always through run.sh: nvrs_cuda is an MPI program and launching it bare
-- initialises no transport, so UCX complains and the process exits before it
-- has looked at the deck.
--
-- Norne is METRIC.  NVRS works in field units internally and converts on the
-- way in, then reports the answer back in the deck's own units -- so the
-- pressures printed here are bar, not psi.
-- ===========================================================================

TITLE    NORNE (OPM open benchmark) -- corner-point, faulted, 36 wells

FIELDDECK  dataset/NORNE/NORNE_ATW2013.DATA

-- Phase pressure gradients, bar/m.  Equilibration cannot derive these without
-- PVT tables and will not guess them, so they are stated.  These are the
-- values Norne's own equilibration file documents in its header.
GRADOIL    0.0706
GRADWATER  0.1001
GRADGAS    0.0186

-- Connate water for the hydrocarbon columns.  Only a fallback now: with
-- PROPSARRAYS below, the deck's own per-layer SWL is used instead, which runs
-- from 0.04 in the top layer to 0.16 in Ile 1.1.
SWCONNATE  0.1

-- Take the field deck at its word.
--
-- PROPSARRAYS reads the per-cell arrays that live in PROPS and REGIONS, which
-- the grid reader used to stop before.  That is what makes ENDSCALE's
-- endpoints visible, and it also hands equilibration the deck's own SWL and
-- EQLNUM -- Norne has five equilibration regions, and the field is initialised
-- against the record each cell is assigned rather than against the first one.
--
-- USEENDSCALE then scales each cell's relative permeability onto its own
-- endpoints, honouring SCALECRS YES (three-point).  This is the term that
-- decides when water starts to flow: the deck sets SWCR = SWL + 0.08, so
-- water is immobile below a saturation of 0.12 to 0.24 depending on the
-- formation.  Without it every cell uses the table's own critical water, and
-- water moves earlier than the deck says it should everywhere at once --
-- which is what left NVRS producing 2.31x OPM's water and 0.69x its oil.
PROPSARRAYS  1
USEENDSCALE  1

-- Drive the wells from the field deck's 247-step schedule.
USESCHEDULE 1

-- Flowing bottom-hole pressure for the producers [psi].
--
-- Norne is history matched: its WCONHIST records state what each well
-- produced and default the bottom-hole pressure -- all 2,109 of them.  This
-- simulator's producers are pressure controlled, so a value is needed, and
-- without one those wells would be handed a BHP of zero and pull the entire
-- reservoir pressure across every completion.
--
-- The FLOOR a producer may pull to, used only where the deck states no BHP
-- limit of its own.  Norne's WCONHIST history records mostly do not, so this
-- applies to nearly every producer.
--
-- It was 2900 psi, and that was throttling the whole field.  Against an
-- initial 3774-4543 psi it leaves under 900 psi of drawdown, so the wells sat
-- on the floor instead of on their rate targets -- visible in the report as a
-- bottom-hole pressure pinned flat at 2900 across B-2H, B-3H, D-1H, E-1H,
-- E-3AH and E-4AH while Flow's swings down to 500-2000.  A well held above
-- its target under-delivers every phase at once, which is why oil, water and
-- gas were all low together.
--
-- 500 psi is a floor rather than an operating point: the rate targets bind
-- first, and this only catches a well that cannot meet one at all.
PRODBHP  500

-- Crossflow in a rate-controlled producer.
--
-- 0 (the default, and what this run uses): a producer's flowing pressure is
-- solved so that only the completions above it carry the rate target; any
-- completion below it is shut for the step.  This is OPM's own !allow_cf
-- branch in StandardWell::computePerfRate.
--
-- 1 restores the previous behaviour: one division over every completion, which
-- lets the ones below the flowing pressure subtract from the total.  That is
-- neither of OPM's branches -- OPM's reverse-flow path uses TOTAL mobility
-- distributed by the wellbore mixture, so what goes back in always carries the
-- well's own composition.  Applying each phase's local formation mobility
-- instead let B-4H and D-4H take oil in at one completion while pushing gas
-- out of another, which is why they reported large negative oil beside large
-- positive gas in the c755f1e comparison.
--
-- 2: allow reverse completion flow, as WELSPECS defaults to, but carry
-- the wellbore's own mixture on it.  This is the physical OPM branch;
-- unlike 1 it never pushes a cell's local phase composition backwards
-- through a common wellbore.
--
-- Set to 1 to reproduce that run exactly.
WELLXFLOW 2

-- Pressure ceiling for an injector whose WCONINJE record defaults one.
-- 600 bar = 8702 psia, which is what Norne's own records state for the
-- wells that do give a limit.  Without it those wells inject their full
-- stated rate whatever the cell pressure does: the gas residual then
-- holds a term independent of the unknowns and Newton freezes on it.
INJBHP   8702

-- Matrix-free eliminated common-BHP coupling for rate-controlled wells.
-- Mode 7 uses the complete [BHP, oil fraction, water fraction] block whenever
-- reverse flow makes the wellbore mixture an active reservoir unknown.  On an
-- all-forward well it uses the more robust frozen-mobility pressure column;
-- there is then no reservoir-side mixture column to eliminate.
-- Mode 8 is the earlier one-sided injector allocation experiment.  Mode 9,
-- retained here, replaces it with Flow-style injector crossflow and eliminates
-- the complete [BHP, oil fraction, water fraction] injector block.  A
-- high-pressure completion can produce reservoir composition into the well;
-- lower-pressure completions reinject the resulting wellbore mixture together
-- with the scheduled wellhead stream.
WELLSCHUR 9
-- OPM-style black-oil primary switching: the third primary is Sg in a cell
-- holding free gas, Rs once the gas has gone, Rv once the oil has gone.
-- Corner-point (FIELDDECK) grids only; see the manual.
PVSWITCH 1

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------- fluids
NCOMP    3
MODEL    BLACKOIL
OUTDIR   OUTPUT_NORNE
CASENAME NORNE

-- Which well curves the report draws.
--
--   WELLPLOT ALL                  every quantity
--   WELLPLOT WOPR WWCT WGOR       only these
--   WELLPLOT NONE                 skip the section
--
-- Known: WOPR (oil rate), WWPR (water rate), WGPR (gas rate), WWCT (water
-- cut), WGOR (gas-oil ratio), WBHP (bottom-hole pressure).  One figure per
-- quantity with every well overlaid, since the question these answer is which
-- well is behind the field's behaviour.  Absent means ALL.
WELLPLOT ALL

-- Viscosities [cP] and formation volume factors, representative of Norne's
-- PVT at reservoir conditions.
--
-- These are FALLBACKS.  With PVTCONST 0 below, the run reads PVTO, PVTG,
-- PVTW and DENSITY out of the PROPS section of the deck FIELDDECK names, and
-- these constants are then used only for the AIM throughput estimate.  Set
-- PVTCONST 1 to ignore the tables and run on these numbers instead, which is
-- what makes a table-versus-constant comparison a one-line edit.
UW       0.32
UO       1.20
UG       0.02
BW       1.0113
BO       1.3360

-- Bg in a FIELD deck is rb/scf, not the deck's own rm3/sm3.
--
-- Norne's PVTG gives about 0.00456 rm3/sm3 near reservoir pressure.  Putting
-- that number here unconverted makes every scf of gas occupy 5.6 times the
-- reservoir volume it should -- which for a gas injector at 1.4 million
-- sm3/day into two completions is the difference between a hard step and an
-- impossible one.
--   0.00456 rm3/sm3 x (6.28981 rb/rm3) / (35.3147 scf/sm3) = 8.12e-4 rb/scf
BG       0.000812

-- Dissolved gas [scf/stb].  Norne's RSVD is about 120 sm3/sm3 at the datum;
-- x 5.6146 gives scf/stb.  The tabulated saturated value at 4018 psi is 704,
-- so this is a good constant -- but a constant is exactly the problem: with a
-- frozen Rs, dRs/dp is zero, and injected gas can neither dissolve into the
-- oil nor compress.  With the tables in play RS becomes the anchor for the
-- UNDERSATURATED branch only: the Rs an oil keeps where no free gas exists
-- to saturate it.  Where gas is present the bubble-point line sets it.
RS       674

-- PVTCONST picks the deck's constants over the built-in correlations, and
-- must stay 1 here.  Clearing it does NOT enable the tables -- PVTTABLES does
-- that -- it hands r.rs, r.bo, r.bg and r.ug to correlations tuned for a
-- generic fluid, whose Rs is about 5650 SCF/STB against Norne's 674.  The
-- constants above then stop describing this field, and the anchor for the
-- undersaturated oil goes with them.
PVTCONST 1

-- Read PVTO/PVTG/PVTW from the PROPS section of the deck FIELDDECK names.
-- 0 to ignore them and run on the constants above, which is what makes a
-- table-versus-constant comparison a one-line edit.
PVTTABLES 1

-- Phase densities as SPECIFIC GRAVITIES, not lb/ft3.  The gravity term is
-- GRAVGRAD * rho, and GRAVGRAD (0.4335 psi/ft) is already the gradient of
-- water, so rho here is relative to water.  Norne's DENSITY record gives
-- 1033 / 859.5 / 0.854 kg/m3, which divided by 1000 is:
RHOW     1.033
RHOO     0.8595
RHOG     0.00085

-- Total compressibility [1/psi]: rock plus fluid.
--
-- This is not a refinement, it is what makes the problem solvable.  With
-- PVTCONST the formation volume factors are constants, so this is the only
-- pressure dependence in the accumulation term.  Set it to zero and the
-- reservoir is exactly incompressible -- and Norne's first year is depletion,
-- three producers and no injector, which an incompressible closed reservoir
-- simply cannot do.  The pressure operator degenerates to a singular Neumann
-- Laplacian, the linear solve stalls at a fixed residual, and Newton fails at
-- every timestep however small.
--
-- 2e-5 /psi is a normal total compressibility for a consolidated sandstone
-- with live oil.
CFO      0.00001
-- Rock compressibility [1/psi].  With the tables in play this is what it
-- says: the pore volume opening up.  With PVTCONST 1 it has to stand in for
-- the fluids' compressibility too, and is then some nine times too small for
-- gas -- Norne's tabulated gas gives 1.8e-4 /psi at 4200 psi.
CT       0.00002
-- SUPERSEDED for this case.  The field deck carries its own ROCK,
-- 4.84e-5 /bar at 277 bar, which is 3.34e-6 /psi, and that is now read
-- and used in place of the value above.  The run says so on startup.
-- 2e-5 was a generic sandstone figure standing in for a number the deck
-- had all along; it is six times too compressible for Norne, and OPM
-- Flow has been using the deck's own value throughout.
-- Global connate water and residual, used by the Newton saturation clamp and
-- the Appleyard chop.  With PROPSARRAYS/USEENDSCALE these are OVERRIDDEN from
-- the deck's own SWL and SWU arrays, because a single pair for the whole field
-- contradicts them: SWI 0.10 against cells SWATINIT puts at 0.08 makes the
-- clamp push those cells up every iteration while the equations pull them
-- down, and Newton freezes.  Left here for the non-ENDSCALE path.
SWI      0.10
SWR      0.10
PBUB     3900
PATM     14.6959

-- PINIT/SWINIT are the fallback only: with GRADIENTS given, the initial state
-- is equilibrated from each region's datum instead.
PINIT    3900
SWINIT   0.20
SGINIT   0.05
SOINIT   0.75

GRAVITY  1

-- ------------------------------------------------------- saturation functions
SWOF
 0.0000  1.0000  0.0000
 0.1000  1.0000  0.0000
 0.2000  0.6000  0.0050
 0.3000  0.3400  0.0180
 0.4000  0.1700  0.0450
 0.5000  0.0700  0.0900
 0.6000  0.0200  0.1600
 0.7000  0.0030  0.2500
 0.8000  0.0000  0.3600
 1.0000  0.0000  0.4000
/

SGOF
 0.0000  1.0000  0.0000
 0.0500  0.7000  0.0050
 0.1000  0.4500  0.0250
 0.2000  0.2000  0.1000
 0.3000  0.0700  0.2200
 0.4000  0.0150  0.4000
 0.5000  0.0000  0.6000
 0.9000  0.0000  1.0000
/

-- ------------------------------------------------------------ solver
JACOBIAN  ANALYTIC       -- required by AIM, and exact for three-phase
-- Fully implicit.
--
-- AIM decides which cells may run explicit in saturation from a throughput
-- CFL measured on the state at the START of the step -- which is before the
-- schedule has been applied.  On the step where C-4H becomes a gas injector
-- the cells around it are still quiet when that measurement is taken, so they
-- are marked explicit, and then 1.4e7 scf/day of gas arrives in them.  An
-- explicit saturation cannot survive that, and chopping dt does not help
-- because the next measurement is taken on the same pre-injection state.
--
-- AIM 1 restores it, and is sound for a field with no new injectors opening.
AIM       0
CONNLIST  1              -- forced anyway on a corner-point grid

KRYLOV    FGMRES

-- Inexact Newton.
--
-- The first run solved every linear system to 1e-6 while Newton's own
-- tolerance is 1e-3, and spent 194 Krylov iterations per Newton doing it --
-- 66,716 for a single report step, which was the entire cost of the run.
-- There is no value in a Newton direction computed to six digits when the
-- Newton iterate itself is three digits from the solution: the extra accuracy
-- is discarded by the next residual evaluation.
--
-- 1e-4 keeps the direction good enough for Newton to converge at its usual
-- rate at a fraction of the work.  Raise it towards 1e-6 only if the Newton
-- count starts climbing.
LINTOL    1e-4
LINMAXIT  200

-- FGMRES restart.
--
-- At 30, a solve that needs 194 iterations restarts six times and throws away
-- the Krylov subspace each time -- precisely the regime where restarting hurts
-- most.  A larger subspace costs memory (one vector of 133,293 doubles per
-- unit, about 1 MB) and buys back the superlinear convergence that restarting
-- destroys.
RESTART   80
MIXED     1              -- FP32 multigrid preconditioner
EMULATE   1              -- df64 operator

LINSOLVER AMGX
AMGFAST   1
AMGREUSE  10
AMGREUSEGROWTH 2.0

-- An injector must walk its cell pressure to wherever injectivity balances
-- the requested rate -- where WI.lambda.(p_bhp - p) equals q -- and that
-- target is set by the rate, not by the timestep, which is why chopping dt
-- six times changed nothing at all.  For C-4H that is a climb of several
-- thousand psi, and a low cap can abandon a direction which is still reducing
-- the residual.  Twenty iterations was best while a zero-target history-well
-- bug left an artificial water sink in the model.  Once that sink was removed,
-- 30 reduced total Newton work from 3,406 to 3,069 and time from 41.29 to
-- 40.25 s; 40 retained the same 35 chops but rose to 3,273 Newtons and
-- 41.61 s.  NEWTONSTALLMAX still ends genuinely stalled attempts early.
NEWTONMAX 30
NEWTONTOL 1e-3
-- Backtrack the fully implicit Newton update until the residual actually
-- descends, halving alpha at most this many times.  Appleyard and DSMAX
-- already make the update nonlinear in alpha, so each trial restarts from
-- the same state rather than rescaling an already clipped one.  With the
-- accepted trial carried into the next iteration (see main.cu) a search
-- that accepts alpha = 1 costs no extra assembly at all.
LINESEARCH 6

-- Abandon a sub-step after this many Newton iterations that fail to reduce the
-- residual.  A stalled attempt does not recover; the answer is a shorter step.
NEWTONSTALLMAX 4

-- ---- time integration, following OPM Flow ------------------------------
-- Carry one continuous timestep ACROSS report steps and rescale it from the
-- Newton count of the last converged sub-step, as Flow's AdaptiveTimeStepping
-- does, instead of resetting dt on a dyadic lattice at every report step.
DTKEEP    1
DTCONTROL 1
-- Remember a failed length but relax that ceiling after every successful
-- substep, so one isolated switch cannot pin an entire report interval.
DTFAILCAP 0.75
DTCAPGROW 1.1

-- Flow's getReservoirConvergence: exempt a fraction of the pore volume from
-- the strict CNV bound so a handful of near-degenerate cells cannot hold the
-- field hostage.
--
-- Full-history validated Flow-style convergence policy.  The relaxed CNV
-- applies only to the default lowest 3% of pore volume.  With the complete
-- mode-9 well block, 3.5e-5 is the last screened MB tolerance that preserves
-- pressure, BHP, GOR, and phase-rate accuracy together; 4e-5 was rejected.
OPMCONV   1
MBTOL     3.5e-5
CNVRELAXED 10.0

-- These four match their code defaults exactly and are listed for reference
-- only, deliberately commented out:
--   DTRESTART 0.33   DTMAXGROW 3.0   DTGROWDAMP 3.2   DTDECAYDAMP 1.0
-- Chop a saturation update that crosses a relperm endpoint so it lands just
-- past it, rather than letting one iteration leap between mobility regimes.
-- A gas front arriving in a cell at residual gas saturation is exactly the
-- case this exists for.
APPLEYARD 1

-- Pressure and saturation change limits per Newton iteration.  DPMAX is
-- generous because the injector has a long way to climb and every iteration
-- it spends clamped is one it cannot spend converging; DSMAX stays tight
-- because saturation is what actually goes unstable.
DPMAX     1000
DSMAX     0.15
DTGROW    2
-- DTRELAX  4            -- let a comfortable step recover several halvings at
-- DTTARGET  8            --   once, not one.  Left off so this deck reproduces
--                        --   the published Newton counts; see the manual.

-- ------------------------------------------------------------- time
-- Norne's history runs 6 NOV 1997 to 1 DEC 2006.
--
-- 3312 days, not 3304.  3304 is the span from the FIRST REPORT STEP
-- (14 NOV 1997) to the last, which is what the schedule summary prints;
-- TEND is measured from START, eight days earlier.  With TEND 3304 the
-- last two of the schedule's 247 dates fell outside the run and the
-- comparison against Flow was two steps short.
--
-- DTREPORT is not used here: REPORTDATES defaults on, so a field run
-- reports at the schedule's own 247 dates.  It still governs a run with
-- REPORTDATES 0, and a Cartesian deck, which has no schedule to follow.
DTREPORT  30
DTMAX     10
TEND      3312

-- ------------------------------------------------------------ output
ECLOUT       1
MAKECSV      1
MAKESUMMARY  1
